          %title Literature
          %p
            %strong Quantum group Fourier transform
            refers to several variants of Fourier transforms attached to Hopf algebras or their analytic versions.
          %p
            The usual Fourier transform is between functions on locally compact abelian group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            and functions on its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pontrjagin+dual"} Pontrjagin dual
            locally compact abelian group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat{G}
            (the group of continuous characters on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            with the topology of uniform convergence on compact sets), cf. wikipedia,
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pontryagin_duality"} Pontrjagin duality
          %p
            In noncommutative case, the role of Pontrjagin duality is played by a version of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tannaka-Krein+theorem"} Tannaka-Krein duality
          %p
            To avoid analytic details for starters, and still to introduce elements of the general story, one can look at the case when
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            is finite. Then, over a field
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mover
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k \hat{G}
            is isomorphic with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dual+Hopf+algebra"} dual Hopf algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi k
                  %mi G
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (k G)^*
            of the group Hopf algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k G
            which is itself isomorphic as a Hopf algebra to the function algebra
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k(G)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mover
                      %mi G
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                    %mo ≅
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k \hat{G}\cong k(G)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            to the function algebra on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℱ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi h
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi u
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi χ
                      %mo ∈
                      %mover
                        %mi G
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mi h
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi χ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi χ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi u
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathcal{F}(h)(u) := \sum_{\chi\in \hat{G}} h (\chi)\chi(u)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℱ
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi χ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi G
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi u
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi G
                  %mi χ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi u
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi u
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathcal{F}^{-1}(\phi)(\chi) := \frac{1}{|G|}\sum_{u\in G}\chi(u^{-1})\phi(u)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Λ
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi u
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi G
                  %mi u
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi k
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Lambda = \sum_{u\in G} u \in k G
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Λ
                    %mo *
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mi χ
                  %mi χ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Lambda^* = \sum_{\chi} \chi
          %p
            where one assumes that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |G|
            is invertible in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi χ
                  %msup
                    %mi Λ
                    %mo *
                  %mo =
                  %mi ϵ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi χ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msup
                    %mi Λ
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi \Lambda^* = \epsilon(\chi)\Lambda^*
            for all
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi χ
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi k
                  %mover
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi\in k \hat{G}
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Λ
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo =
                  %mi Λ
                  %mi ϵ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Lambda \phi = \Lambda \epsilon(\phi)
            for all
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi k
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi\in k G
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Lambda
            is a right integral
            %em in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k G
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Λ
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Lambda^*
            is a left integral in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi k
                    %mi G
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (k G)^*
          %p One can write
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℱ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi h
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi Λ
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi h
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi Λ
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathcal{F}(h) = \Lambda_{(1)}\langle h, \Lambda_{(2)}\rangle,
          %p
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi h
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi k
                  %mover
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi k
                  %mi G
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h\in k \hat{G} = (k G)^*
            (notice the usage of left
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coregular+action"} coregular action
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℱ
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi Λ
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo *
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                      %msubsup
                        %mi Λ
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 2
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo *
                      %mo ,
                      %mi S
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                      %msup
                        %mi Λ
                        %mo *
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Λ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathcal{F}^{-1}(\phi) = \frac{\Lambda^*_{(1)}\langle \Lambda^*_{(2)},S \phi\rangle}{\langle \Lambda^*, \Lambda \rangle}
          %p
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi k
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi\in k G
            and where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo :
                  %mi k
                  %mi G
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi k
                  %mi G
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mi op
                      %mo ,
                      %mi cop
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S: k G\to (k G)^{op,cop}
            is the antipode. These formulas make sense for more general
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dual+bialgebra"} Hopf algebras in duality
            provided there are appropriate analogues of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Lambda
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Λ
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Lambda^*
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %msup
                    %mi Λ
                    %mo *
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle \Lambda^*, \Lambda\rangle
            is invertible in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            = succeed "." do
              %strong quantum group Fourier transform
          %p
            They can also be related to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+operator"} fundamental operator
            in Hopf algebra
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multiplicative+unitary"} multiplicative unitary
          %h3#literature Literature
          %ul
            %li
              V. Lyubashenko,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Modular transformations and tensor categories
              %strong 98
              (1995) 279–327
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0022-4049(94)00045-K"} doi
          %blockquote
            %p
              For an abelian tensor category we investigate a Hopf algebra F in it, the “algebra of functions” or “automorphisms of the identity functor”. We show the existence of the object of integrals for any Hopf algebra in a rigid abelian category. If some assumptions of finiteness and non-degeneracy are satisfied, the Hopf algebra F has an integral and there are morphisms
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                      %mo ,
                      %mi T
                      %mo :
                      %mi F
                      %mo →
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S, T : F \to F
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              are similar to those of the Fourier transform.
          %ul
            %li
              V. Lyubashenko, S. Majid,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Fourier transform identities in quantum mechanics and the quantum line
              = succeed ";" do
